-- title:
--   Elements of order one at a place are separating
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and suppose $K$ is perfect. Assume given an element $x \in F$ such that $F$ is algebraic over the intermediate field $K(x)$ obtained by adjoining $x$ to $K$ inside $F$ — so $F$ is a function field of one variable over $K$ in this sense. Let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ in the sense of the structure [`AlgebraicCurve.Place`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L22): a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$ under the structure map, distinct from all of $F$, and whose underlying ring is a principal ideal ring. For $f \in F$ write $\operatorname{ord}_v f$ for the integer $-\log$ of the value of $f$ under the $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued valuation attached to $v$ viewed as a point of the height-one spectrum, i.e. the normalised additive order of vanishing at $v$. Let $t \in F$ satisfy $\operatorname{ord}_v t = 1$, that is, $t$ is a uniformiser at $v$. The conclusion is that $F$ is a separable algebra over the intermediate field $K(t)$ generated by $t$ over $K$.
--
--   Classically this says that a uniformiser at a place of a function field of one variable over a perfect constant field is a separating element, the separability criterion behind the construction of differentials in positive characteristic. Within the development it is the source of separability for uniformisers, and is used by the results on the divisor $D$ and the differential coefficients attached to an element of order one at a place; its proof cites the transcendence over $K$ of elements of nonzero order and the fact that $F$ remains algebraic over $K(t)$ for transcendental $t$.
-- source:
